--TEST--
A `private` method publishes its parameters' declared types exactly as a `public` one does, and a method declaring no parameter has no row to ask rather than a row answering nothing — `rule:core-classes/reflect`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A parameter's type is *shape*, not access: it is what the declaration
// published, and no call is made to learn it. So visibility gates reading and
// writing a value and gates nothing here — a description that filtered the
// roster by visibility would answer a `private` method's arity and refuse to
// say what it takes, which is the half-answer this asserts against.
class Say {
    public static function text(?string $value): string {
        if ($value == null) { return "<none>"; }
        return $value;
    }
}

class Vault {
    public function open(string $key): bool {
        return true;
    }

    private function seal(string $key, int $turns): bool {
        return true;
    }

    // Declared, and declaring nothing. Its roster is empty because there is
    // nothing to list, which is the same empty a member no source spelled
    // answers with — and there the *count* is what tells the two apart.
    public function close(): bool {
        return true;
    }
}

var $described = Core\Reflect::forObject(new Vault());

foreach ($described->methods() as Core\Reflect\MethodInfo $method) {
    echo $method->name(), " count=", $method->parameterCount(), " types=";
    foreach ($method->parameters() as Core\Reflect\ParameterInfo $parameter) {
        echo " ", Say::text($parameter->type());
    }
    echo "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
close count=0 types=
open count=1 types= string
seal count=2 types= string int
